      setup) _describe 'action' '('poller:Set this machine up to collect and nothing else: no exposition, no storage, no dashboards. It creates a profile of its own, gives the collector an identity distinct from every other, writes the SNMP community where the helpers read it, generates the collector unit and starts it, and says whether any of it will survive a reboot. Repeat --watch to give it work in the same breath. Everything it does is a command you could run yourself; none of it is a decision only you can make.' 'local:Set this machine up to collect, store, and draw: collector, exposition, VictoriaMetrics, and the dashboards. Everything binds to this machine only unless --publish says otherwise, which needs --confirm because it serves your readings and dashboards to anything that can reach that address.')' ;;
      traffic) _describe 'action' '('list:Show every monitored target with its recent activity as a sparkline.' 'watch:Monitor one or more interfaces on a switch over SNMP. Every name is verified against the device before it is accepted, and a comma-separated list is resolved in one SNMP walk. Watching the first port on a switch also starts collecting that switchs own health -- uptime, CPU, memory, temperature, and rejected management access -- which stops again when the last port on it is unwatched.' 'ping:Monitor round-trip time and loss to an address from this collector.' 'subscribe:Mark a link as one you pay for, and record what it is sold at. Marking it is what puts it on the overview as its own tile and includes it in the WAN total; the rate itself is kept as a soft limit -- nothing is enforced, and nothing is discarded for exceeding it. The tiles read in bits per second rather than as a share of the contract, so a wrong figure here costs you the soft limit and not the reading. --facing says which way the port looks: isp (default) is the port that reaches the provider, where what the switch receives is your download; lan is the port facing inward, where it is the other way round. --clear removes the subscription.' 'name:Give a monitored port a name of your own for dashboards -- "AFRIX" rather than Gi1/0/16 -- and optionally pin its colour with --color, so it is the same colour on every chart instead of whatever the grapher assigns by arrival order. Colours: teal, orange, green, violet, or an explicit #rrggbb. Those four were checked together for separation, lightness, and colour-blind readability; a fifth of your own was not. The name is not part of a series identity, so naming a port that has been collected for weeks keeps its history rather than starting a new series. Everything after the target is the name, so it needs no quoting to contain spaces. --clear removes it and the port shows as the device calls it again.' 'push:Send this collectors current readings to a VictoriaMetrics import endpoint. They arrive as the counters they are, carrying this collectors id, so they stay distinguishable from the receiving machines own readings and every dashboard reads them unchanged. This is the other way a poller hands its readings over: pulling fetches them over SSH and needs a path inward, pushing needs a path outward, and a network usually permits one or the other. The receiving machine must have been told to accept it -- storage binds to loopback until setup local --accept-push --confirm says otherwise.' 'dashboard:Print every dashboards URL and say whether the grapher is answering. save keeps a dashboard you have rearranged in the browser, writing it in place of the generated one so it survives the next stack init; revert goes back to the generated version. Dashboards are generated because a generator keeps five of them consistent and hand-edited JSON does not, but a board you have arranged the way you read it should not have to be given up for that. --open launches the overview in a browser. When it is not answering this says which of the two reasons it is -- the service is not started, or the machines per-user systemd manager is not running at all -- because from a browser those look identical and lead somewhere quite different.' 'health:Show switch-level readings for every switch with a watched port: uptime, control-plane CPU, memory, temperature, and open sessions. These are collected automatically alongside the ports, because a port that went quiet reads differently depending on whether its switch rebooted or ran out of CPU. A dash means the switch does not answer that OID; nothing is wrong. Failed SSH logins are in no standard MIB -- read the switchs syslog for those.' 'unwatch:Stop monitoring one or more targets and discard their samples. A phrase matching several targets needs --confirm.' 'pause:Stop collecting a target without discarding its history. A running collector notices on its next tick.' 'resume:Start collecting a paused target again.' 'interval:Change how often a target is collected, between 10 and 3600 seconds.' 'status:Show whether a collector is running and when each target last produced a sample.' 'interfaces:List the interfaces SNMP reports on a device, with index and description.' 'poll:Collect one round from every enabled watch. Intended for cron as well as by hand.' 'daemon:Collect continuously until interrupted, taking each target at its own interval. --interval overrides every targets interval for this run. Polls only what is on the watchlist.' 'exporter:Publish collected readings as Prometheus exposition for VictoriaMetrics to scrape. Counters are raw and monotonic; rates are derived at query time. Binds loopback, answers GET only, and runs no command. --once prints the body and exits.' 'backfill:Import history collected before the metrics stack existed into VictoriaMetrics. Stored rates are published as netrune_history_* gauges, kept separate from the live counter series so a derived value is never read as a measured one.' 'stack:Write the VictoriaMetrics scrape config, vmalert rules, dashboards for the selected grapher, systemd user units, and a compose file. Dashboards are provisioned from these files rather than edited in a browser. graphers lists which are available. --role collector generates the collector alone -- no exposition, no storage, no dashboards -- which is what a remote poller needs: the machine that pulls from it keeps and draws the readings, and anything published on the poller would be a new listener on the network it was placed there to observe.' 'export:Print this collectors stored readings as JSONL for another install to pull. Read-only, and limited to what this collector measured: a poller does not pass on what it pulled from somewhere else.' 'pull:Merge a pollers readings into this install. --remote fetches them over SSH; --file merges an export the poller wrote, which is what to use where SSH between the two subnets is not permitted -- an export is a file, and anything that can move a file will do. - reads standard input. A file is merged whole rather than by window, and merging is idempotent, so re-reading one adds nothing. Without --since a fetch resumes from the newest reading already merged, so a repeated pull carries only what is new.' 'remote:Manage remote pollers, and run any traffic command on one over SSH. Anything that is not add, list, or remove is forwarded verbatim to the real CLI on the poller, so remote control and local control cannot drift apart.' 'verify:Walk the pipeline link by link and report where it breaks: watchlist, collector, exposition, storage, scraping, rate derivation, and grapher. Checks run in the order data flows, so the first failure is the cause rather than a symptom.' 'report:Write a self-contained HTML report with charts for the collected window.' 'show:Show throughput or latency as terminal sparklines with current and peak values. The window accepts seconds or a duration such as 15m, 6h, or 2d.')' ;;
      path) _describe 'action' '('inspect:Plan from cached topology, then validate VLAN, trunk, STP, LAG, SVI, VRF, route, ARP/ND, and policy evidence where supported. `netrune ghoster` is an alias.' 'findings:Query findings across every recorded path run, newest first, with a frequency summary. Answers which switches or interfaces keep failing rather than what one run said.' 'history:Show chronological path runs newest first with reusable compact run references.' 'show:Reopen a persisted path by full ID, compact history reference, or latest endpoint/VLAN match.')' ;;
      excel) _describe 'action' '('inspect:Report which rows match which Netrune switch and what is out of date, without writing. --free-ips also checks the unallocated addresses (pinging them), so the report shows which are free. Finds each sheets header row and columns, matches rows by serial, base MAC, IP, or hostname, and lists conflicts and unmatched rows. --sheet picks the sheets to read by name (any case) or by number, comma-separated for several; the heading numbers every sheet.' 'sync:Write Netrunes current hostname, IP, manufacturer, model, serial, base MAC, and platform into the matched rows empty or out-of-date cells (status and last_seen with --fields). --refresh FIELDS rewrites those whole columns on every matched row, even cells that already agree in another notation and even under --fill-only, and stops them voting on which switch a row is. Each written cell is shaded by confidence in five steps: green certain (serial/MAC matched), blue strong (two keys agreed, or a new row), yellow fair (one IP or name filled a blank, or a sheet serial/MAC replaced), tan weak (one IP or name overwrote a value), red doubtful (--prefer broke a conflict); a switch not seen for 30 days drops one step; --no-shade turns it off and --comments adds a note with the old value. --remarks writes one brief note per row into the Remarks/Comments/Notes column (added when missing), worded as the techs own: what changed, Offline, Check: keys disagree, Duplicate of row N; the operators own text is kept. --free-ips (implies --remarks) marks addresses Free to use: never while a device Netrune knows (online or offline) or one seen in the last 30 days holds it; an empty row (no hostname, serial, or MAC) is free without pinging; a row naming something Netrune never saw is pinged and, if silent, asked about under --confirm in a terminal (y: Free to use (old entry), n: Allocated, offline) or settled with --accept-free/--reject-free IPS|all. The report suggests addresses for allocation, empty rows first. A remark saying reserved/DHCP/gateway is respected, and a Free to use is replaced once the address is in use. Formula cells, other columns, and other sheets are left alone. --confirm backs the original up, saves atomically, reads the file back to verify, and records an audit event; without it nothing is written.' 'export:Write a new workbook: an Inventory table, a live Summary of formulas and a chart, and a Migration Tracker with dropdowns, lookups, and overdue highlighting. --confirm is needed only to replace an existing file, which is backed up first.' 'report:Write a workbook of fixed-definition reports, one sheet each: active-aps, active-switches, transitions (renames, IP changes, IPs now on another device, serial/model changes, AP moves), switch-history and ap-history (every device online or offline, plus each replaced piece of hardware). Changes cover the last 30 days unless --since widens or narrows them. Each reports exact definition is written on the Report Info sheet. --confirm is needed only to replace an existing file, which is backed up first.')' ;;
      support) _describe 'action' '('bundle:Package version, redacted settings, integrity, drivers, plugins, and recent run summaries. Inventory and raw evidence are excluded.')' ;;
    esac
  else
    _arguments '*:argument:'
  fi
}
_netrune "$@"
